<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russo-Persian War (1826–1828)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1826">1826</a>–<a href="/years/1828">1828</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russo-Persian War of 1826–1828 began when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, led by Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a>, attacked Russian-held territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a> to recover lands lost in 1813. After early successes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> were defeated at Ganja, and the Russians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich">Ivan Paskevich</a> took Erivan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a> of 1828 ceded the khanates of Erivan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and made the Aras river the border.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1826-1828.jpg" alt="Russo-Persian War (1826–1828)" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Литография Г. Беггрова по оригиналу В. Машкова · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_near_Elisavetpol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war was the last major military conflict between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and Qajar Iran, fought over territorial disputes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a>. Iran had controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> since 1502 and saw it as a natural extension of its land. The earlier war of 1804-1813 ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a>, by which Iran conceded the khanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a>, Ganja, Shakki, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Quba</a>, Shirvan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> and part of Talish, and abandoned its claims over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. The frontier was unclear, and the region between Lake Gokcha and Erivan remained the most disputed. In 1825 Russians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksey-yermolov">Aleksey Yermolov</a> seized the northern bank of Lake Gokcha, and Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a> protested.</p><p>At Fath-Ali Shah's court a peace party and a war party lobbied the shah. The war party, led by Agha Sayyed Mohammad Esfahani and including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a>'s adviser Abol-Qasem Qa'em-Maqam II, argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was weak, citing Yermolov's recent defeats and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decembrists">Decembrist revolt</a>. The Council of Soltaniyeh gathered to advise, and the decision was for full-scale war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s entry in the summer of 1826 began well, for Yermolov's men were unprepared and the Muslim population rose against the Russians; garrisons were driven out or withdrew in Shirvan, Ganja, Talish, Shakki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>, apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shusha">Shusha</a>. Abbas Mirza besieged Shusha from 6 August, but his artillery could not break through, and Colonel Iosif Reutt held out with the help of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>. On 16 September Valerian Madatov defeated Amir Khan Devellu-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> at Shamkor, and Ganja surrendered to him the next day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich">Ivan Paskevich</a> arrived on 22 September and took command from Yermolov on 29 September.</p><p>At the Battle of Ganja, on 13-17 October, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a>'s army of about 30,000 broke up after he ordered two of his young sons evacuated, which split its centre. By 1827 he was defending. In July Paskevich defeated him and the fortresses of Abbasabad and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordubad">Ordubad</a> surrendered, and in September Erivan fell after a siege and bombardment. On 24 October Tabriz surrendered without a fight, though Asef al-Dowleh had 20,000 men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> was captured and plundered on 25 January 1828.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 2 February 1828 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a>, ceding Erivan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhichevan</a> and agreeing to heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>. It stripped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> of its last territories north of the Aras, comprising all of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, the Nakhchivan Autonomous Republic and Iğdır Province, and the shah now needed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s acknowledgment of the heir he named. After the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> state never again faced Russia on equal footing or was treated as an equal by European countries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russo-Persian War (1826–1828)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Russo-Persian War</b> ended with the Treaty of Turkmenchay in 1828, the Aras became the border, and Qaradagh remained within Iran as a governorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-qaradagh">Khanate of Qaradagh</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Russo-Persian War</b> of 1826-1828, Ehsan Khan Kangarlu went over to Russia, and the Treaty of Turkmenchay in 1828 ceded the khanate to the Russian Empire, which joined it with Erivan in the Armenian Oblast.</blockquote><a href="/state/nakhichevan-khanate">Nakhichevan Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
